  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Drab Tooth (Phellodon fuligineoalbus) is a small, inconspicuous fungus in the Bankeraceae family characterized by its pale, drab coloring and tooth-like structures beneath its cap. Like other tooth fungi, it bears small spines on the underside of its fruiting body rather than gills or pores.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Spines/Teeth",
      "description": "Distinctive small, pointed spines on the underside of the cap that run down the stem",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Color",
      "description": "Pale grayish-white to drab gray cap with whitish to pale spines",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Typically small fruiting bodies, usually less than 2 inches across",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found on rich forest floors and among mosses and leaf litter in coniferous and mixed woodlands throughout New England.",
